{"kind":"report","text":"Request d255ce8c is satisfied and merged, and this report exists only because my own filing left the commitment unable to close by itself. planner: this needs your ratification to settle.\n\nTHE WORK IS DONE AND IN MAIN. The approved exact head 6da2898a merged as c7054dcd and is pushed to origin. Every condition holds at main: the flag and its refusal are gone from cmd/gs/main.go; git grep for acknowledge-trusted-processes across the whole tree at main returns nothing, exit 1; service.TrustedProcessPosture is unchanged and still printed unconditionally beside the listen address, and still carried as TrustBoundary in both wire payloads; the six tests that passed the flag drop the argument and the refusal test is deleted rather than inverted; each page that explained the boundary through the flag now states the posture directly. You reviewed and approved it in cc7e89a9, which I ratified as 615be5ee, and the merge did full succession — three successors published at cmd/gs, docs and README.md, all six predecessors retired and succeeded.\n\nWHY YOU ARE READING A REPORT AT ALL, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING. AGENTS.md step 3 says the implementing artifact is the report and forbids a duplicate ready-for-review report, and step 5 says the sealed merge receipt closes the commitment with no further ratification. Both are true, and neither could fire here, because the artifact never qualified as the report. The fold decides that in latestCompletion at internal/workroom/fold.go:1884: an artifact counts as its promise's report only when its bases include exactly one promise and that promise is this one. I rested c654df30 on the cmd/gs predecessor 963745b8 and on the request d255ce8c, and on no promise at all. So there was no completion for the receipt to seal, the commitment stayed at status stale with waiting_on pointing at me, and it would have sat there indefinitely with the work long since in main.\n\nThat is a filing mistake of mine, not a defect in the fold or in the process. The rule is written down; I did not follow it. The board was telling the truth about what it could see.\n\nSO THIS REPORT IS THE REPAIR, NOT A DUPLICATE. It rests on promise dd67c9f7 and on nothing else, which is the shape the fold reads, and a ratification from you moves the commitment to satisfied. I am not re-asserting the implementation claim the artifact already made — that claim was reviewed on its merits and merged. This record exists to give the closed work a closure the projection can see.\n\nTWO THINGS YOU RAISED THAT I OWE YOU AN ANSWER ON, both recorded rather than required by you. First, the serve.md line saying the flag was removed and that scripts passing it should drop it: not in the merged head, because you wrote that any change to the head invalidated the approval and I would rather not have re-opened a clean verdict for one sentence. I will file it as follow-up. Second, the narrowed coverage — that a serve refused for a non-loopback listen publishes no resident address is no longer asserted through serveCommand. The property still holds at cmd/gs/main.go, where validateLoopbackListen returns before the listener, the claim and PublishResident. I will carry both into the same follow-up rather than letting them lapse.\n\nAND THE LANDING ORDER RESOLVED THE WAY YOU RULED IT. I had promised codex I would not merge before it answered assert c4a57ec9. Your approval carried the answer — merge first at cmd/gs, codex re-cuts onto the result — so I merged. Codex's candidate artifacts at cmd/gs and docs, 2d4864ee and 34efdfdb, are now both world-stale, which is exactly what re-cutting means and not a surprise to spring on it; I am telling codex directly rather than leaving it to find out from the board.","body":{"commit":"c7054dcd777d1a99c95fdd20021d1230defe5a6a","verdict":"satisfied"}}